---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { posts, blog } from "../../data/posts";
import { book, retailers } from "../../data/site";
import { getBlogPostingSchema, getBreadcrumbSchema } from "../../utils/schema";

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === "fifteen-thousand-people-five-hours")!;

const schemas = [
  getBlogPostingSchema({
    slug: post.slug,
    title: post.title,
    description: post.description,
    datePublished: post.date,
    ogImage: book.ogImage,
    wordCount: 2900,
    keywords: post.tags,
  }),
  getBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: blog.name, path: "/blog/" },
    { name: post.title, path: `/blog/${post.slug}/` },
  ]),
];

const amazon = retailers.find((r) => r.name.toLowerCase().startsWith("amazon"));
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${post.title} — ${blog.name}`}
  description={post.description}
  lang="en"
  ogType="article"
  publishedTime={post.date}
  articleType={true}
  jsonLd={schemas}
>
  <article class="essay">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="pt-8 text-xs text-[color:var(--base-ink)]/55">
      <a href="/blog/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-[color:var(--base-prime)]">
        {blog.name}
      </a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
      <span>Essay</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="essay-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Essay</p>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">{post.subtitle}</p>

      <p class="byline">
        By Fran C. Wood
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        <time datetime={post.date}>{post.dateLabel}</time>
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        {post.readingMinutes} min read
      </p>

      <div class="figure">
        <span class="figure-num">75,000</span>
        <span class="figure-cap">
          hours of human life in a square on a Friday afternoon. Laid end to
          end, without sleeping, that is eight and a half years of continuous
          time.
        </span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="essay-body">
      <p class="lede">
        Fifteen thousand people. Five hours each. That is a medium-sized climate
        demonstration.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the question I asked myself — crude, probably unfair — was this: what
        if those same fifteen thousand people spent that energy living
        differently instead?
      </p>

      <p>
        I don't mean five hours. I mean sustained attention. Paying attention to
        what they eat. To what goes into the water when they wash their hair,
        when they wash their clothes. Asking, before buying a shirt, whether it
        is actually needed or whether there are already three in the wardrobe
        doing exactly the same job. Choosing a brand for what the brand does
        rather than what the brand says. Small things, no drama, no cameras,
        nobody watching.
      </p>

      <p>Which one weighs more?</p>

      <p>
        For a while I was convinced that question had an answer, and that the
        answer was uncomfortable for the people marching. I was wrong — though
        not entirely. The question was badly posed. The discomfort, on the other
        hand, was legitimate. It was just pointing somewhere else.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">01</span>Why the question is badly posed</h2>

        <p>
          The mistake is a category error. A demonstration is not trying to
          reduce the emissions of the fifteen thousand people who show up. That
          was never the point. Its mechanism is different, and it is indirect:
        </p>

        <p class="chain">
          fifteen thousand people <b>&rarr;</b> a visible event <b>&rarr;</b> media
          <b>&rarr;</b> public conversation <b>&rarr;</b> political pressure
          <b>&rarr;</b> a law <b>&rarr;</b> millions of people
        </p>

        <p>Whereas living differently works like this:</p>

        <p class="chain">
          fifteen thousand people <b>&rarr;</b> fewer flights, less meat, fewer
          cars, less consumption <b>&rarr;</b> fewer emissions
        </p>

        <p>One is an arithmetic. The other is a bet.</p>

        <p>
          Comparing the two by “how much CO<sub>2</sub> does each hour save” is
          like comparing a seed to a lever. Individual action is measurable,
          small and certain. Collective action is unmeasurable, uncertain and
          potentially enormous. They are not on the same scale because they are
          not doing the same job.
        </p>

        <p>
          So the question is not which one yields more. It is a different
          question, and it took me a while to find it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">02</span>Do demonstrations work? Yes, sometimes, and in strange ways</h2>

        <p>
          Worth saying before getting sceptical: there is evidence that protests
          move things.
        </p>

        <p>
          Start at the beginning of the chain, which is attention. King, Bentele
          and Soule studied civil rights issues in the US Congress between 1960
          and 1986 and found that protest activity — together with the perceived
          legitimacy of an issue and competition from other issues — helped
          explain how many congressional hearings were devoted to each one.
        </p>

        <p>
          It is worth reading carefully what that result does and does not say.
          What is measured is <em>legislative attention</em>, not legislation.
          Protest can get a problem onto the agenda of the people who decide.
          That is not nothing — almost nothing gets onto that agenda — but it is
          not a law either.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then there is the German case, which is far more specific. Fabel and
          colleagues assembled close to four thousand climate protests from
          2019, used mobile-phone mobility data to build a very fine-grained
          measure of turnout, and matched it against nine hundred and sixty
          electoral districts. Districts with higher Fridays for Future turnout
          saw larger increases in the Green vote share. And they found evidence
          for three mechanisms: reverse intergenerational transmission of
          environmental attitudes — from children to parents — more Green
          politicians talking about climate on social media, and more
          environmental coverage in local media.
        </p>

        <p>
          The ifo Institute estimated that around 7% of the increase in the Green
          vote between 2019 and 2021 could be attributed to the protests: 0.45
          percentage points of a 6.3-point rise. That is an estimate, not a
          settled measurement, and worth treating as one. But it gives a sense of
          the scale: real, and modest.
        </p>

        <p>
          And now the study that dismantled my original argument, which is
          Italian. Marini and Nocito analysed ten million used-car transactions
          before and after Fridays for Future mobilisations in Italy, using
          rainfall on the day of the protest as an exogenous source of variation
          in turnout — if it rains, fewer people go, and that variation should
          not be related to later car-buying preferences. After the
          mobilisations: fewer cars bought, lower average emissions among those
          bought, more low-emission vehicles and fewer high-emission ones.
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
          The demonstration ended up changing consumption decisions. The whole
          dichotomy collapses.
        </p>

        <p>
          But here is a twist that forces me to correct myself, and it is more
          interesting than my original reading. The authors tested two possible
          mechanisms: an increase in environmental awareness, and rational
          anticipation of stricter future regulation. The second appeared to be
          generally more important.
        </p>

        <p>
          In other words: people did not buy cleaner cars because the protesters
          inspired them. They bought cleaner cars because they read the protest
          as a signal that the state was coming to tighten the rules, and they
          did not want to be holding an asset about to lose value.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is colder and less romantic than what I wanted it to say. It is
          also more solid. And it reinforces precisely the political chain —
          protest, expectation of regulation, behaviour — over the chain of
          contagion and good example. I note it here because it will come back,
          and because it cuts against my own thesis.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">03</span>And yet: visibility is not effectiveness</h2>

        <p>
          If the essay ended there it would be a nice story and also a lie by
          omission.
        </p>

        <p>
          Gethin and Pons examined the quasi-universe of US protests between 2017
          and 2022 — fourteen protest waves covering the environment, gender,
          race, guns, immigration and domestic and international politics — and
          measured Twitter activity, Google searches, public opinion, voting
          intention, election results, politicians' behaviour, and policies
          proposed and adopted.
        </p>

        <p>
          The title of the paper says a good deal:
          <em>The Limited Effects of U.S. Protests</em>. They found large effects
          on internet activity and minimal effects on everything else.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is worth having tattooed somewhere:
          <strong>something going viral does not mean anything changed.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          There is worse. Vandeweerdt ran two pre-registered experiments — in
          Denmark, with activists blocking a road; in the UK, disrupting a
          sporting event. Exposure increased the likelihood that people named
          climate change as one of their country's most important problems: +19%
          in Denmark, +10% in the UK. But there were no significant effects on
          climate concern or on intention to act, and a small backlash did appear
          against the protesters' specific policy demand.
        </p>

        <p>
          The paper's title puts it better than I can: climate disruption buys
          salience, and it pays for it.
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
          Salience is not persuasion. You can get everyone talking about the
          problem without getting anyone to agree with your solution.
        </p>

        <p>
          Even the counterintuitive finding has fine print. A study in
          <em>Nature Sustainability</em> examined the Just Stop Oil campaign and
          found that greater awareness of that radical group was associated with
          increased identification with and support for Friends of the Earth, a
          moderate group that took no part in the actions. This is the
          <em>positive radical flank effect</em>: the radical flank makes the
          moderate one look reasonable by contrast.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here too I have to correct myself. It would be too categorical to say
          that later research “showed” the effect is temporary. What we actually
          have is evidence that <strong>radical flank effects can be positive or
          negative depending on political context, audience characteristics and
          how the protest is perceived.</strong> A 2025 review finds both kinds.
          And 2026 research suggests that actively distancing oneself from
          radical groups can increase support for moderates.
        </p>

        <p>
          The honest conclusion is neither “protest works” nor “protest doesn't
          work.” It is that it depends on how, when, against whom, with what
          concrete demand, and inside what strategy. A demonstration can be
          enormous, moving, photogenic — and change nothing at all.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">04</span>The other half: is changing your life enough?</h2>

        <p>No. And this needs saying with the same bluntness.</p>

        <p>
          A study of the potential of individual action in a Finnish suburb
          concludes explicitly that voluntary individual efforts alone are not
          sufficient to reach a carbon budget compatible with 1.5 °C: behavioural
          change has to be combined with strong policy and technological
          progress. It is a single suburb and should not be extrapolated to the
          planet, but as empirical evidence of the ceiling on voluntary action it
          does the job.
        </p>

        <p>
          The European <em>1.5° Lifestyles</em> project studied mobility,
          housing, food and consumption across five countries. It found that
          moving away from car-based mobility — especially combustion cars —
          carries one of the largest reduction potentials, followed by housing,
          particularly renewable heating. But its underlying conclusion is the
          one that matters here: meaningful individual change requires
          transformation of the political-economic, technological and social
          structures that make those lifestyles possible or difficult.
        </p>

        <p>You cannot cycle through a city that has nowhere to cycle.</p>

        <p>
          What did weaken is one of the favourite arguments against individual
          action: the idea that tending to your own habits works as an
          anaesthetic, that recycling makes you feel so good you stop demanding
          anything. A longitudinal study followed 2,778 people across four annual
          waves of an Australian survey between 2021 and 2024. Within a given
          year, personal climate actions were associated with greater collective
          participation and, to a lesser extent, greater support for climate
          policy.
        </p>

        <p>
          And here I correct myself a third time, because the precise formulation
          matters. The defensible finding is <strong>that personal action was not
          found to crowd out collective action.</strong> Not that it produces it:
          the longitudinal models found little evidence of systematic carry-over
          from one year to the next.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is less than I would have liked. But it kills the anaesthetic
          argument, which was the one that mattered.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">05</span>How we learn, which is what I was actually asking</h2>

        <p>
          Here is the heart of it, and it has nothing to do with tonnes of
          CO<sub>2</sub>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Humans learn by watching. We do not learn because someone explains
          something well. We learn because we see someone do something and see it
          go well for them. It is an ancient mechanism, older than language, and
          it is still the most powerful one we have.
        </p>

        <p>
          And that is where the real difference between the two paths shows up —
          not a difference of efficiency but of grammar.
        </p>

        <dl class="contrast">
          <div>
            <dt>A demonstration says</dt>
            <dd>“We have to change.”</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>A life says</dt>
            <dd>“Look, this is what the change looks like.”</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <p>
          Those are two completely different speech acts. One is a demand. The
          other is a demonstration in the older sense of the word. And
          demonstrations move humans in a way that demands do not.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then there is the slower, deeper level: social norms. Someone starts
          buying clothes only when they need them. A friend does the same. Their
          children grow up with that logic already inside them, without anyone
          explaining it. Another friend starts asking “do I actually need this?”
          And slowly what counts as <em>normal</em> in that group shifts. Because
          people do not only ask what is right. We ask, almost without noticing,
          <strong>what do people like me do.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          And here comes the turn I did not expect: the demonstration also works
          by imitation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Someone sees fifteen thousand people marching and thinks “there are a
          lot of people who think this way.” That changes their sense of what is
          normal. It is exactly the same psychological engine — social influence
          — running in a different format. The German study found precisely this:
          children shifting their parents' attitudes. Interpersonal influence,
          not a placard.
        </p>

        <p>
          The difference is bandwidth and proof. The march reaches many
          strangers, through a screen, for one day, and reaches them as an
          assertion. Your life reaches your sister, your children, your three
          closest friends, the people you work with, for years — and it reaches
          them with the evidence attached.
        </p>

        <p>
          Which produces the one thing that by now seems to me beyond argument:
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
          If the new behaviour looks like a sacrifice, it does not spread.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is no way around this. You do not build a mass movement by
          offering people a worse life. If the implicit message is “give up
          comfort, pleasure, status and wellbeing for the planet,” the capacity
          to spread is structurally limited, however right you are. You can be
          completely correct and completely unimitable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whereas if someone looks at you and thinks “this person lives well,
          seems calm, is healthy, is not broke, and consumes a third of what I
          consume,” the implicit message is different and it is devastating:
          <strong>I could do that too.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          That is not moralising. It is the only thing that has ever moved
          populations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">06</span>The uncomfortable conclusion</h2>

        <p>So the conclusion I arrived at, and don't entirely like, is this:</p>

        <div class="thesis">
          <p class="eyebrow">The thesis</p>
          <p>
            Go to the march. But if your life is not the thing you claim to be
            fighting for, stay home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          It is ugly. It sounds like moral policing. And yet it has a force I
          find hard to dismantle, because a demonstration whose participants go
          home to live exactly the life they are denouncing is asking society to
          do something none of them is doing. And people read that. Not
          consciously, not as an argument — but they read it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Put as provocatively as I can manage: <strong>the most powerful
          activist may not be the one holding the biggest sign, but the one whose
          life makes someone else want to change theirs.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          Now I have to do that idea the work it deserves, which is to try to
          break it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">07</span>The three arguments that nearly destroy it</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>First: it is the hypocrisy trap.</strong> “Greta took a plane.”
          “You use a phone made of minerals.” Demanding personal purity as a
          condition for speaking is the favourite weapon of people who want
          nothing to change, precisely because it is an unsatisfiable condition.
          Nobody lives coherently inside an industrial economy. Nobody. If
          coherence is the entry ticket, nobody enters, and the demand
          disappears — an outcome that suits exactly one side.
        </p>

        <p>
          And there is something worse. The individual-responsibility frame did
          not arrive on its own. Here precision matters, because the version that
          circulates is false: <strong>BP did not invent the carbon
          footprint.</strong> The ecological footprint concept dates from the
          1990s and the term had earlier uses. What is documented is that
          <strong>BP played an important role in popularising the idea of the
          <em>personal</em> carbon footprint</strong>, through its “Beyond
          Petroleum” campaign and an online calculator launched in 2004.
        </p>

        <p>
          The difference between “invented” and “popularised” looks minor and
          isn't: the first version is false and disqualifies you, the second is
          verifiable and is enough for the argument. Which remains brutal: the
          most effective thing the oil industry ever did was convince you the
          problem was your shower.
        </p>

        <p>
          My conclusion flirts with that argument. It has to be said without
          anaesthetic.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Second: it is a class argument.</strong> “Living in harmony” is,
          to an uncomfortable degree, purchasing power and geography. Buying
          unpackaged, eating well, choosing non-toxic products, living somewhere
          you can walk or cycle, having time to cook — much of that is money and
          time. Turning personal coherence into a requirement for political
          participation makes activism the property of those who can afford it.
          And it excludes precisely the people who suffer most from climate
          change, who tend to have the smallest footprints and the narrowest room
          to manoeuvre. Telling someone renting a flat with insulation they
          cannot change, in a city with no transit, working two jobs, to stay
          home until they have sorted their life out is grotesque.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Third: the evidence does not support it — but less than I
          thought.</strong> Here I have to be honest against myself twice in a
          row.
        </p>

        <p>
          In Germany the Green vote rose where turnout was higher, and the study
          never asked whether those protesters lived coherently. Its evidence
          points to other mechanisms: media coverage, politicians' communication,
          transmission of attitudes from children to parents.
        </p>

        <p>
          I wanted to use that as proof that personal coherence is irrelevant. It
          isn't. <strong>A study not measuring a variable does not demonstrate
          that the variable doesn't matter.</strong> It simply did not evaluate
          it. This is the weakest of the three arguments, and it is better to say
          so than to hide it behind a clever line.
        </p>

        <p>
          What does survive is more modest and more interesting: a protest can
          produce measurable political and social effects without our knowing
          anything about the coherence of the people protesting. An imperfect
          messenger does not void the message. That is not the same as saying it
          makes no difference who carries it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">08</span>What survives</h2>

        <p>
          Understanding those three arguments does not oblige me to treat them as
          tied with mine. They have to be weighed.
        </p>

        <p>
          And I think the distinction that saves the idea is this:
          <strong>personal coherence as an entry requirement is indefensible;
          personal coherence as a multiplier is the most underused thing in
          climate activism.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          As a requirement — as a moral condition on the right to speak — the
          thesis is wrong and should be dropped without nostalgia. It is wrong
          strategically, because it silences the movement. It is wrong ethically,
          because it expels the most affected. And it leans on a frame the fossil
          industry helped install.
        </p>

        <p>
          As a multiplier, it holds. Your life is the highest-bandwidth channel
          you have with the people who actually know you. If your life does not
          support what you are asking for, it is not that you lost the right to
          ask: it is that you left your best tool in the drawer.
        </p>

        <p>
          But there is a version of the original provocation that survives
          intact, and it is still uncomfortable — only the accusation is a
          different one.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a form of activism that is, at bottom, identity. Going to the
          march as self-image, as belonging, as a moral purchase: five hours that
          buy you a year of feeling on the right side. That activism does not
          fail because it is hypocritical. It fails because it is oriented toward
          the participant's self-perception rather than toward any mechanism of
          change. It optimises for the feeling, not the effect. And it is easy to
          recognise: it measures nothing, it never asks what worked last time, it
          repeats the format because the format is the point, and it goes home
          without anything having moved — outside or inside.
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
          That is what my question was actually pointing at. Not hypocrisy.
          Self-reference.
        </p>

        <p>
          And I include myself, for what it's worth. I am writing this having
          taken more flights than I can justify and having bought things I did
          not need last week. I am not making the accusation from outside. There
          is no outside.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="snum">09</span>The circuit</h2>

        <p>
          The two mechanisms are not rivals. They are a circuit, and each does
          what the other cannot.
        </p>

        <p>
          Collective action is what makes sustainable living <em>possible</em>.
          It builds the bike lane, insulates the housing, changes the energy mix,
          takes the toxic product off the shelf. And — according to the Italian
          study — it does something more interesting still: it changes people's
          expectations about where regulation is heading, and that alone changes
          what they buy. Without it, living well is a sport for the disciplined
          and well-resourced.
        </p>

        <p>
          Personal coherence is what makes collective action <em>credible and
          contagious</em>. It is the channel through which the demand stops being
          a reprimand and becomes a plausible life.
        </p>

        <p>
          But they are not symmetrical, and saying “both matter” and stopping
          there would be exactly the comfortable draw this essay set out to
          avoid.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you had to pick one for humanity, you pick political action, without
          hesitating. It is the only mechanism that operates at the scale of the
          problem. Collective action has a very high ceiling and a floor of zero.
          Individual action has a low ceiling and a guaranteed floor.
        </p>

        <p>
          Except you are not humanity. You are one person. And for one person the
          question is not which mechanism is more efficient in the abstract, but
          which one you can sustain for years and which one actually moves
          somebody near you.
        </p>

        <p>
          So the question is not whether you have earned the right to march.
          March. The question is more uncomfortable than that:
        </p>

        <p class="pull">
          If someone who knows you well looked closely at your life — not your
          opinions, your life — would they want any of what they see?
        </p>

        <p>Because if the answer is no, the placard is not going to fix it.</p>
      </section>

